# 0003. Synthetic English and Russian conversations; memory is the last 3 exchanges

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-19

The model's memory is its input: the last 3 exchanges plus the new message. The training
data is generated so the right answer depends on what is inside that window, which teaches
it both to remember and to admit it has forgotten.

## Technical details
- **Input**: `context_tokens(history, message)` = `<u> user <b> bot` for each of the last
  `MEMORY = 3` exchanges, then `<u>` + the new message. When it is longer than
  `MAX_CONTEXT`, the oldest tokens are dropped. The target is the reply wrapped in
  `<start> … <end>`. Anything older than the window is gone.
- **Data**: about 10,000 conversations of 3-10 turns, half English, half Russian, from
  fixed lists (`NAMES`, `FOODS`, pets, cities) and ~40 canned replies:
  - chit-chat (greetings, "are you a robot?", jokes in order): one reply per phrasing,
    so there is one right answer to learn;
  - telling a fact (name, favourite food, pet, city);
  - asking for it later: if the fact is inside the window, the reply uses its latest value
    (corrections work); if not, "hmm ... i don't remember . tell me again ?".
- **Language**: one shared vocabulary and model; the bot answers in the language of the
  message. Added in #1, alongside English.
- **Consequences**: memory is exactly 3 exchanges; names, foods, pets and cities must come
  from the lists. The Telegram bot strips `!` from messages because the data never has it.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Alternatives: a real dialogue corpus (no control over what is remembered, needs far
  more training); an explicit memory store or slot filling outside the model (the point is
  to see attention find the fact); a longer window (longer inputs, slower training).
